Resumption of the very American ideal that labour rights at the same time are human rights
Congressmen Keith Ellison and John Lewis have proposed a law designed to protect organization in unions as civil law.
"Focuding the way unions operate, the middle-class jobs work," says Ellison, a Democrat senator for Minnesota co-chief of the congressional committee.
That's why I'm so proud to be able, together with the pattern of occasional rights John Lewis, to introduce a law reinforcing the empowering of employees.
This groundbreaking legislature will give workers the same legal opportunities to defend themselves against discrimination when organising in trade unions as in other forms of non-discrimination - and halt anti-defunitarian forces
Advance law on labour relations so as to allow all employees who face discrimination if they want to engage in trade unions, salute in a civil court - and judging compensation or damages - an initiative is correct and necessary.
Certainly it is not a radical initiative - at least according to U.S. standards.
The best way to comprehend Ellison, Lewis and other appellants in the law is to re-adhere to the U.S. ideal.
Despite trade union struggles that have taken place in recent years - Wisconsin, Michigan, and other states - Americans have once encouraged countries around the world to embrace, expand and respect labour rights.
There has been a time, and millions of Americans still remember when the country in one breath promoted democracy, freedom of speech, the freedom of the press and the right to assembly.
When the United States occupied Japan after World War II, Gen. Douglas MacArthur and his advisers supported the country to adopt a constitution designed to ensure Hideki Toya's military autocracy being replaced by democracy.
They were convinced that employees and their trade unions played a role in shaping a new Japan, incorporating the language and openly acknowledging that “engassie’s right to assemble, collectively bail out and action is guaranteed.”
When the United States occupied Germany after World War II, General Dwight David Eisenhower and his advisers urged the Germans to form a constitution that would ensure Adolf Hitler's fascism would be replaced by strong democracies.
Negotiators were aware that employees would need to be organized and promoted in a new Nation, so they included a clarification that openly says: "The right to create associations that will secure and improve working and economic conditions will be guaranteed to every individual and every profession or profession.
Negotiations that will restrict this right or seek its damage will be announcable. Measures leading to the fulfillment of such a purpose are unlawful.
When former First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt presidized the International Commission on Human Rights, which proposed the Universal Declaration of Human Rights adopted by the United Nations in 1948 as a global treaty, the petitioners and Roosevelt included a guarantor that “Everyone has the right to create trade unions in order to protect their interests.”
For generations, Americans have embraced the fundamental premrigor that labour rights are human rights.
Advising other countries on how to create a civil and democratic society, the Americans explained that the right to create labor unions - and their involvement in collective bargaining with firms and government agencies in the position of an equal partner - must be protected.
Now that these rights are being invaded in America, it is certainly necessary to go back to the American ideal that working people must have the right to organize themselves in a free and open society.
Fifty-year-old Reverend Martin Luther King Jr. said:
History is a great teacher.
Today, everyone knows that trade unions have not diminished the strength of the nation, but expanded it.
They increased the standard of living of millions of people, creating a market for the industry and bringing production to an unsuspected level.
Those who attack the trade unions forget these simple truths, but history remembers them.
History doesn’t forget, and we wouldn’t.
Formal recognition of labour rights as a human - and extension of the protection of civil rights so as to prevent discrimination in organizing trade unions - is delayed.
Keith Ellison and John Lewis rebuild the ideals that have lifted America and have made it a real promise of democracy.
