Israeli children go back to school after war in Gaza
Thousands of Israeli children from areas close to Gaza returned to school on Monday after spending the summer in anti-bomb shelters while rockets and mortars attacked their homes during the fifty-day war between Israel and Hamas. However, schools in Gaza remain closed while the territory is recovering from the fighting.
The beginning of the school brought a feeling of joy and arousal to the communities in the south of Israel marked by attacks, but the signs of the fighting are still noticeable.
In Ashdow, a city in the south of the country, nursery workers hit by a rocket have removed the remains of shrapnel from the wall and slowly await the return of the children.
"We're a little scared, but otherwise we look forward to it," said Ronit Bart, a resident of Kibuc Saad and an English teacher at a local school.
A lot of children in this area really need to go back to a daily routine.
Her eleven-year-old daughter, Shani Bart, revealed that "it's a little odd" to go back to school.
"We've had some difficult moments and we didn't leave the house at all," she said.
Kibuc, located close to the border with Gaza, President Reuven Rivlin was visited to offer its support to residents.
As long as it didn’t stop the fire last week, thousands of border settlement residents like Saad remained inside their homes or left their homes and traveled to safer areas away from Gaza to escape missiles and mortars.
Many Nachal Oz residents, a village close to the border with Gaza, in which a Palestinian mortar missile killed a four-year-old boy, he hesitates to return.
The Minister of Education stated that about twelve families still did not come back.
Their children were temporarily accommodated in surrogate schools.
Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu visited a school in Sderot, a city on the border with Gaza, which was heavily affected by Palestinian fire.
Encouraged children to study diligently, adding: "We will provide you with education and safety."
Israel and the Hamas have reached an indefinite measure last Tuesday.
Stopping fire led to an immeasurable culmination of the fighting, it did not solve key issues as a demand by Hamas for an end to the Israeli-Egypsystadt blockade of Gaza and the reopening of airports and ports in Gaza.
Israel is demanding the disarmament of Hamas and the reinstatement of the bodies of the two soldiers killed in the war.
It is expected that a new round of circumstantial negotiations will begin later this month in Egypt.
Estimates of Palestinians and the United Nations have killed more than 2,100 Palestinians, three-and-a-half of whom were civilians and at least 494 children.
Israel disputes these numbers and estimates that at least half of those slain were militants, even though they do not have clear evidence for these allegations.
On the Israeli side killed 66 soldiers and six civilians, including a Thai worker.
Hamas and other militants from Gaza fired 4,591 missiles and mortar missiles during the fighting on Israeli cities, mainly in the south of the country.
In turn, the Israeli army carried out more than 5,000 aerial raids and other attacks.
Israeli attacks have damaged or devastated thousands of houses in Gaza, and an estimated 250 000 people have hid in a hundred U.N. schools that have erected into a makeshift shelter.
Due to the tens of thousands of people who still remain in the hide-and-season, school clerks have postponed the start of the school year last week.
"I'm hoping they're going to relaunch the school soon so that we can finish our education as well as Jewish children and other children in the world," Mohammad Amara, a thirteen-year-old boy from a school in Gaza's largest city.
