According to highly surveyed statistics, the prices of flats in winter have risen the most in the last seven years.
According to the housing value index in the Australian city of RP Data CoreLogic Hedonic, housing prices rose 1.1% in August, the agency said on Monday.
The increase led to an overall increase for June, July and August at 4.2%, the highest increases in the winter month since 2007.
The annual increase in prices climbed to 10.9%, more than twice in one year to August 2013, but the profit was not evenly distributed in the country.
RP Data research director Tim Lawless said Sydney and Melbourne are running in the market.
RP Data figures show Sydney housing prices rose 16.1% last year, compared to 11.7% in Melbourne.
Adelaide, Brisbane and Darwin among the other strongest markets averaged five-and-a-six percent.
On the other side of the schiaphasized Canberra, which was harmed by state-supplied spending cuts and where prices rose by only 1.4% during the year.
