4 tips on how to take better photos and videos underwater
If you're interested in taking photos or shooting underwater, you've got a large selection of equipment at your disposal.
The cheapest option is a compact such as Nikon Coolpix AW120 or an outdoor GoPro Hero3+ Silver Edition, which costs about $300.
I took these photos at a family celebration of several cameras, they all cost less than $350.
It doesn’t matter what kind of equipment you’re using, but there are a few rules to achieve the best results.
Re-Check your equipment.
Even if you have a waterproof camera, make sure that the batteries and other parts are tightly enclosed.
In addition, adjust the camera according to what type of photos or videos you will take.
Some cameras and camcorders have a scenic or squeaking mode that optimizes the exposure to dark under-evoltage.
And before you get into it, find out how deep you can dip with your equipment.
Some cameras are designed only up to 5 feet, others up to 50 to 60.
Recognise our guide to buying and evaluating digital cameras in both normal and waterproof design.
Always take a few pictures - a lot of them don't work out.
Computed cameras have an LCD, which will help you compose photos, but outdoor ones don't.
Even if you have an LCD, underwater it will be difficult to see and the composition of the shot will be unpredictable.
So always take more pictures.
If the camera has bracketing mode, which performs a series of images with a slightly different dispersion of exposure, take advantage of it.
Stay close to the surface.
It's declining significantly with a deeper dive of light.
If it is possible to stay close to the surface when taking photos in the pool, lake or sea.
This also allows you to capture more light in the photos - the deeper you sank, the fewer colors you will see.
Relaunch close to the photographed object.
That's an excellent piece of advice if you're photographing on land, but it's even more important because of more muted light.
It is especially important if you are filming with an outdoor camera: Such devices usually have a solid and wide angle of the lens, which means that if you want to fill the photo frame, you need to get closer to the objects.
Andrew Lawson was a man whose power of personality was able to change things even in such a huge organization as NHS.
As an anaesthusologist, he dedicated his career to relieving patients from the pain caused by illness and treatment.
His wife recalls that one of the people who sought his help was an MI6 officer who had to live with the aftermath of the torture that murmured.
Lawson understood that while doctors are concentrating on diagnoses and illnesses, treated patients are overwhelmingly interested in something completely different: pain.
But one day in 2007, it was he who began to suffer.
"I don't feel myself," he wrote in May that year.
I felt without energy.
He struggled with symptoms of the flu and found that he was impatient and his wife Juliet involves remorse.
“I want everything to happen sooner rather than later.”
When Juliet rode a business trip for a week, Lawson was unexplicably and unexplacably agitated.
Something wasn't all right.
A colleague gave him an X-ray of his chest.
One two weeks earlier, he was skiing in the French Alps.
X-ray results have arrived.
He had mesotelioma, an incurable tumor that afflicts pleiers, or a lining of the lungs.
It's hard to determine what was the cause.
Although some smokers get lung cancer, not all patients with lung cancer have smoked.
Mesotelioma is different.
In almost all cases it is the cause of asbestos exposing - a building fibrous material that was previously referred to as "miraculous", but today it is known that it is deadly dangerous.
It wasn't hard for most of us to ignore Mesotelioma.
Asbestos, after all, belongs to the past.
Asbestos-harvest is not used in the UK from the 60's, when a ban on asbestos use in the industry came into force.
Even at a time when asbestos was in use, only certain groups of people came into close contact, such as isolation bombers, builders, carpenters and workers in shipyards.
Toxin used in the industry in a different era today finds it difficult to suspect.
Such compliment, however, is not appropriate.
As it turns out, a mesothelioma outbreak culminates in Britain.
The number of mesotelioma deaths is higher here than in any other country in the world.
2,500 deaths from the disease a year is twice as much as the death toll from automobile accidents.
Number of mesothelioma death deaths a year since 1980 and estimated deaths in the future in the UK
The only reason we’ve been feeling its effects right now, even though asbestos use has been illustrative for years (all types of asbestos were eventually banned by law in 1999) is that it usually takes several decades for mesotelioma to undevelop.
Disaster in the form of a Mesothelioma does not only concern veterans of the construction industry.
Asbestos was, and in many cases it is still, present in the homes in which we live, in the offices where we work, in the schools where we learn, and in the shops in which we shop.
Mesotelioma therefore disrespects the social strategation, wealth, vocation or age.
Privileged bastions from London department stores to public schools don't represent a hide-and-season.
Parliament, too, is full of asbestos.
Even the hospitals in which we should be treated are a repository of this lethal carcinogen.
Andrew Lawson isn't old.
He didn't even impose isolation.
He was trying to figure out where he could come into contact with asbestos.
Then he figured it out.
"It is possible that a lot of asbestos has been in Guy's Hospital basement corridors where I've spent six years on practice," he wrote.
Everyone - students, nurses, doctors and gatekeepers - used the corridors.
The question, how many of my contemporacies get the same illness?
Unfortunately, it was a question he was able to partially answer.
“Of the four physicians who have been practiced at Guy’s Hospital and who then had mesotelioma in the past five years,” he said in a letter in 2010, “I’m the only one still alive.”
How many of us get sick?
Andrew Lawson was diagnosed with mesotelioma when he was 48.
When he died on February on February 17 this year, he was 55.
Surviving with this diagnosis for so long isn’t usual.
55 percent of people diagnosed with mesothelioma will die within eight months of diagnosing.
Illness is always deadly.
So now we can only repeat Lawson’s question: “Which of us gets the same sickness?”
According to a leading British mesothelio expert, Professor Julian Pet, the best estimate is that between 1970 and 2050, when an asbestos outbreak is taking place in Britain, about 90 000 people will die.
Most of them currently do not have any idea that they will die in this way.
