Why the universe can have multiple dimensions and we don't see them
Physicists don't usually write bestsellers, but Brian Green of the University of Columbus managed to hit black in 1999.
“His Elephant Universe has become the most accessible path for lay people to intrude on the gosssury of problems, which is now dealt with by a number of theoretical physicists—an quest to unify two well-performing but uncomparable discoveries of the 20th-centen-century: Quantum Theoretice and Theory of General Relacture.
Undoubtedly, one of the causes of the success of the Elegant Universe is that in search of a unified physical theory, it is not a boring story.
Physicists have dealt with the basic problem between the two key theories at the moment (at the moment our universe is de facto divided into two worlds with different laws) and solves so imaginative that it is worth retelling even to lay people.
In addition, Greene dealt with the intricate material so lukewarm and eye-cautiously that he could not be reproached how much interesting he did not include in his book.
In addition, his "mistake" was mostly corrected in two other books: The Structure of the Universe and The Hidden Reality.
Apparently even the author would not have argued if his book became obscured more quickly, but the fact is that even after 15 years the Elegant Universe still provides almost everything important that an informed layman with the ambition to understand the world should know about this important part of theoretical physics.
It is quite probable that in the following years it will be a little more vivid in this area (for the fact that the LHC accelerator will finally go "hot gas"), and so it is a good time to enter the unviolently open doors of the world of modern physicist.
How many dimensions can our universe have?
The imaginary idea that our universe has perhaps more than three spatial dimensions can certainly sound foolish, franchise, strange or mystical.
Nevertheless, it is concrete and completely acceptable.
In order to understand this, let us avert for a moment our eyes from the universe as a whole to something more subliminal, specifically a long and thin garden hose for watering.
Supplied the hundred-metre-long garden hose from one side of the canyon to the other and watch the whole scenery from a half-kilometre distance (as in the picture and on the next page).
From such a distance, you can easily record a long in a horizontal direction of an outstretched hose, but if you just do not suffer from aubrasiveness, you can hardly recognize the thickness of the hose.
Due to its great distance from the hose, you would think that an ant compelled to live on a hose has only one dimension in which to walk: the left-right dimension along the hose.
If someone asks you where the ant was at any given moment, you will only respond to them with just one indication: the distance of an an ant from the left (or the right) end of the hollow.
All we want to say is that from the half-kilometre distance a long pile of hose looks like a one-dimensional object.
In the real world the hose has a thickness.
If we magnify everything, we will suddenly see the second dimension - in the shape of a circle wrapping a hose.
In such an ensuing point of view, it is clear that an ant can actually move in two independent dimensions: in a well-familiar left-rule dimension along a hose longitudinal dimension, but also in the "dimension in/anti-clockwise movement", i.e. around the circular cross-section of the hose.
You are beginning to understand that in order to determine the position of a small ant you have to enter two numbers: how far it is from the end of the hose and where there is a wrapping hose.
This reflects the fact that the hose surface is two-difficult (explain: Experts will point out that this chapter focuses purely on the fault theory of strings.
Difficious aspects are discussing Chapters 12 and 13.).
The hose surface is two-difficult: one long long longitudinal dimension is represented by a direct arrow, the dimension in the circumference direction, denominated by a circular arrow is short and wrigged.
However, there is a clear difference between these two dimensions.
The dimension along the hose is long and easily visible.
The size-wracking perimeter of the hose is short, collapsed and less noticeable.
In order to realize the existence of a circular dimension, we had to examine the hose with a significantly better resolution.
This example illustrates an important feature of spatial dimensions.
Diverified into two groups.
They can either be large, sprawling and therefore directly noticeable or, on the contrary, small, colluded and much less observable.
Of course, in this case, we did not conceive each other in order to reveal the "shit" dimension wrapping the thickness of the hose.
It was enough to bring binoculars for help.
But if the hose had been thinner - like a hair or a capillary, we'd only be able to uncover a shroubling dimension with a lot of effort.
Kaluza released in 19-1919 Einstein an article in which he came out with the stunning idea that the spatial geometry of the universe could have more than three of us-familiar dimensions.
Kaluza justified his radical assertion by providing an elegant and persuasive framework in which Einstein's general relativity and Maxwell's electromagnetic theory can be flowed into a single, unified conceptulic structure.
Immediately the question arises as to how this Kaluz observation goes together with the obvious fact that we can see exactly the three dimensions of the space.
The answer Kaluza silently presidized between the lines, which was clearly pronounced and clarified by Swedish mathematician Oskar Klein in 1926, stands and recedes that the spatial geometry of our universe can have both large and coroned dimensions.
This means that, as well as the dimension in the direction of the hose longitudinally, our universe has a large, vast and slightly visible three dimensions whose existence we are aware at any given moment.
But by analogy with the circular perimeter of the garden hose, the universe can also have additional dimensions, firmly coiled into the space so tiny that they have so far remained hidden even from our most perfect experimental apparatuses.
In order to get a clearer picture of the essence of Kaluz's remarkable proposal, let's stay with the hose for a little longer.
Imagincule that on the circumference of the hose we draw a relatively dense series of circles in black paint.
Disadvantally, the hose still looks like a thin one-dimensional line.
With the binocabulary, thanks to the drawing, we will now reveal a writy dimension even more easily, because we will see the theme from Figure 2.
We can clearly see that the hose surface is two-difficiency, with one dimension large and almost unrestrained, with the other short and circular.
Kaluza a Klein came up with the idea that our universe has a similar structure, but in addition to one small circular dimension, it has three large spatial dimensions, a total of four space-dimension.
Difficult to draw an object with too many dimensions.
In order to help your imagination a little, let's note the illustration in Fig.3; it shows two great dimensions and one small circular dimension.
In the picture, we magnify the view of the geometry of the space, in a similar way as we magnified the hose surface.
Each subsequent level represents a huge magnification of the geometre of space from the previous level.
Our universe can have additional dimensions (we can't see an enaurlation on the fourth level) if they're shrivelled into a sufficiently small space; this explains that we haven't directly observed them yet.
Squad network depicts commonly known "great" dimensions, compared to a new, tiny and coiled dimensional.
Preciately as thread loops in a densely woven rug, even these circles exist in every place of the usual dimensions - we have just drawn them into intersections in a square mercenary.
The background of Figure 4 depicts the commonly known structure of space - an ordinary world around us - on such common scales as meters, depicted by the side of a small square in a square network.
In each subsequent picture, we will focus on the small area of the previous image; we will enugree it so that it becomes visible.
At the very beginning, nothing special happens, as we can see the magnification on the first few levels.
However, when we proceed on our way behind the microscopic properties of the geometry of the space further - to the fourth level of magnification in Figure 3 - we will suddenly see a new, shredded dimension in the shape of a circle, similar to the loops of the thread in a densely wrinkled piece of carpet.
Kaluza and Klein came up with the idea that an additional circular dimension exists in each place in the direction of large dimensions similarly as a circular perimeter at each point of its length.
For the sake of illustrativeness, we have drawn a circular dimension only at some, regularly spaced points.
Figure 4 Kaluz and Klein's notoriety about the microscopic structure of spatial geometries summarise.
Responsiveness with hoses is obvious, although we also notice important differences.
Primarily, the universe has three large, far-flowing spatial dimensions (of which we have drawn only two), whereas the hose has a large dimension of only one.
An even more important difference lies in the fact that we are now talking about the spatial geometry of the universe itself, not just about an object inside the universe, perhaps our hose.
But the basic idea is the same.
If an intestinal circular shroubling dimension is extremely miniature, recognizable it - as well as the circular circumference of a hose - much heavier than obscuring obvious, large, and sprawling dimensions.
If the size of the additional dimension is small enough, it will in fact be revealed beyond the strength of our state-of-kind magnification tools.
However, the most important thing is that an additional dimension is not a mere rounded bump within the usual dimensions, as a two-dimensional illustration erroneously suggests.
Circular dimension is a new dimension that exists at each point of three common vast dimensions.
It's a dimension on the remaining three dimensions independent in the same place, just as the dimensions from above-down, from the left-to-leisure, and front-to-be-be-responsional (and perpendicular to each other).
Sufficiently small ant could move in all four dimensions, and we'd need four figures to determine its position, in addition to the three usual position in a circular dimension; if we're also counting the time, the data five, in any case one more than we would normally have expected.
