To our surprise, therefore, we find that although we are aware of the existence of only three dimensions of space, Kaluz and Klein’s reasoning show that the existence of additional coiled dimensions does not preclude, if they are small enough.
The universe can easily have more dimensions than we can see with the naked eye.
How small should they be?
State-of-frequant technical equipment can recognize the structures of a large billionth of a billionth of a metre.
We can't hardly see any smaller spiked dimensions.
In 1926, Klein comraded Kaluza's original idea with a few ideas from emerging quantum mechanics.
Its calculations indicated that an additional circular dimension could have the size of approximately one Planck length, i.e. a size far beyond the distinctive ability of today's instruments.
Since then, physicists have called the possibility of an additional tiny spatial dimensions by Kalus-Klein theory.
