Genetic Disturbirs Are Often Misdiagnosed
A woman from Britain describes how, after a misdiagnosis for many years, she thought she was going to die.
After being diagnosed with another disease, Karin Rodgers spent most adolescence thinking she only had a few years to live.
In fact, it was Charcot-Marie-Tooth Syndrome (CMT) - a group of hereditary disorders that damage the nerves outside the brain and spine.
Charitable CMT UK reported that a bad diagnosis is a common problem among people with CMT, because very little is known about the disease.
Approximately 23,000 people are believed to suffer CMT in the United Kingdom, which can be manifested by symptoms in the field of motor skills such as musculoskeletal weakness, clumsy gait and twisted toes.
Disabilities or achidation or pain may also occur, this condition is untreatable and progressive, which means that over time the symptoms deteriorate.
When Rodgers was thirteen, she thought she was suffering from Freidreich ataxia (FA) - a disease that has a very poor prognosis.
She believed that before celebrating her eighteenth birthday, she'd been confiscated into a wheelbarrow armchair, and she'd been dead before she was thirty.
The mother-of-treater, who is now 51, says: "As a child, I knew I couldn't do the same things as the others.
I've fallen down every day and it took me a long time.
I've never been able to ride roller skating or skateboards with other kids, and at school they bullied me because of the way I've dated and running."
When Rodgers was t thirteen and after several surgeries that were supposed to release her Achille tendon and straighten her feet, she took advantage of the fact that her doctor had left the room and peered into her dossier, where she had learned that she was suffering the FA.
“I feel guilty because I was snoring, so I didn’t tell anyone, but when I got back home, I searched for information about the disease in the library and I wrote to the FA Association,” she said.
When they responded, I was shocked, I completely panicked.
I've thought I'd end up in a wheelchair before my eighteen, and I'd be dead before I was twenty-five, and in the meantime I'm going to gradually lose all my powers.
“I was totally on my own and I was planning a funeral.”
When she was seveneteen, she realised she didn't deteriorate as much as she had assumed, and she asked her surgeon.
He just stood up, hugged her and said, "Grey, I don't think you've got the disease, otherwise you'd be in a wheelchair.
“I think it’s going to be a lot less life-threatening.”
After graduating from several genetic tests, it was discovered that he had CMT.
"When he told me what CMT was, I thought I'd be a good kid," she said.
Charitable CMT UK has launched a Month of CMT information to draw attention to the disease.
