Heroes from comics
Even a red rug in front of the festival palace has not yet besieged such mobs as in the past and it has supposedly ceased to be a last-minute problem to get a place in a hotel at the last minute.
Crisis is talked about in Italy more than elsewhere, and it is not surprising that it has been afflicted by such esteemed institutions as Biennale and the oldest film festival in the world.
Fortunately, the crisis does not manifest itself as a lack of personalities, but those present solely in connection with the films.
While the younger fans longed for autographs between comic book superheroes Michael Shannon and Andrew Garfield, who successfully performed dramatic role-playing roles in their 99 homes, the main event for all generations became a visit to the legendary actor Al Pacino.
He also shone here in two films.
In an out-of-competition story filmed by Oscar-winning Barry Levison based on Philip Roth's novel, Pacino's seventy-four-year-year-year-old has a role close to him: an actor on the pinnacle of fame who suffers from depreciation at the end of his career and personal life.
But to see him on the second day as an oddial owner of a hardware store from a country town in the Manglehorn, shot by David Gordon Green, it was a truly memorable experience.
Volpi's cup for the best arcade performance shouldn't have missed him - but does an artist of Pacinova's format still needed prizes?
Perhaps for him the greatest satisfaction is the love of the audience, and especially the Italian one, because although he was born in New York, Al Pacino profescided proudly to the ground from which his grandparents come.
Points for Italians
After all, it is the Italian film doing very well in Venice this year.
